---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
import '../../../styles/docs-prose.css';
import { publishedFeatureVersion } from '../../../lib/feature-release';

const patternReleaseVersion = publishedFeatureVersion('0.5.0');
---

<Layout
  title="Pattern Library — Varve Docs"
  description="Create and apply reusable pattern definitions in Varve, with documented source, fill placement, and delivery limits."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <main class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Tools / Pattern Library
      </nav>

      <h1 class="site-page-title">Pattern Library</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        Pattern definitions separate a reusable source from the way one object uses it.
        {patternReleaseVersion
          ? ` The experimental workflow is included in published public beta v${patternReleaseVersion}.`
          : ' The controls remain a source-build preview until a published release includes them.'}
        {' '}The guide below describes current behavior and its limits.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Create a pattern from vector artwork</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>In Design, select the vector artwork you want to use as a motif source.</li>
          <li>Open <strong>Pattern Library</strong> in the Paint inspector.</li>
          <li>Optionally enter a name, then choose <strong>Create from Selection</strong>.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Varve copies the selected artwork and its supported dependencies into a pattern definition.
          It leaves the original artwork in the document. The new definition is a separate source;
          changing the original selection later does not update it. Choose <strong>Edit source
          motifs</strong> on a vector definition to open a draft with a repeated preview and
          per-motif Translation X, Translation Y, and Rotation controls. Click or drag a motif in
          any visible repeat copy, including a dimmed neighbor, to select and move the same source
          motif. A drag can cross the tile boundary; it updates the canonical source instead of
          creating a second motif. The motif list supports arrow-key nudging. Done commits the
          shared source in one history step after it is confirmed, and Cancel drops the draft
          without undoing unrelated work.
        </p>
        <p>
          This compact editor works on top-level copied motifs. It does not yet edit path points,
          nested children, text content, paint, or raster pixels, and it is not a full canvas tool
          session.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Create or import another source</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>New pattern</strong> creates a checkerboard definition with a saved procedural recipe.</li>
          <li><strong>Import tile</strong> accepts PNG, JPEG, WebP, GIF, and AVIF files. Imports are limited to 64 MB and 64 megapixels, and the image data is embedded in the document.</li>
          <li><strong>Replace source</strong> on a library entry changes its shared source to an imported raster tile. Every linked fill sees the replacement; make one fill unique first to isolate that change.</li>
          <li>The library shows each source kind, cell dimensions, and current use count. Search filters by name.</li>
          <li><strong>Edit definition settings</strong> changes the shared name, cell size, gap, mirror state, and grid/half-drop/brick arrangement for every linked fill.</li>
          <li><strong>Export source tile</strong> downloads one SVG cell with embedded source artwork or image.</li>
          <li><strong>Export repeating supertile</strong> downloads a rectangular SVG period for Grid and the conventional Half-drop or Brick arrangement, including mirror parity. Custom stagger fractions are refused when a finite rectangular period is not supported.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Importing a tile does not make its pixels editable as vector artwork. A repeating preview
          does not guarantee that the source edges meet seamlessly. Raster definitions include seam
          inspection with whole-pixel horizontal and vertical offsets. Applying an offset cyclically
          moves the existing texels into a new embedded PNG; it changes the tile phase, not its
          content, and cannot synthesize a missing edge or repair a discontinuity. Offsets are
          limited to tiles up to 16 megapixels.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Apply a definition and place its fill</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Select one or more target objects.</li>
          <li>In <strong>Pattern Library</strong>, use the apply control on the definition you want.</li>
          <li>Select a target and open its pattern fill controls to adjust that fill.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The available numeric placement fields are:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Tile width</strong> and <strong>Tile height</strong> scale this fill’s tile.</li>
          <li><strong>Phase across</strong> and <strong>Phase down</strong> shift this fill’s repeat origin.</li>
          <li><strong>Pattern alignment</strong> anchors the repeat to the object or document/page origin.</li>
          <li><strong>Rotation</strong> turns the field about the selected alignment origin.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For a definition linked from the library, its source and repeat geometry are shared; the
          placement fields above belong to the selected fill. The library’s definition settings
          edit the shared repeat arrangement. Half-drop offsets columns vertically; brick shifts
          rows horizontally. Document/page alignment shares a phase across adjacent objects; PDF
          currently warns and omits this mode because its pattern path remains object-aligned.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Make one use unique</h2>
        <p>
          Select one object that uses a library pattern, then choose its <strong>Make unique</strong>{' '}
          action in the Pattern Library row. Varve creates a separate definition identity for that
          selected object’s use. Other uses continue to reference the original definition. This
          lets you make subsequent source-motif changes independent of the other uses.
        </p>
        <p>
          A definition in use cannot be deleted from the library. Detaching a linked Paint is a
          separate operation; pattern-definition deletion does not silently remove its uses.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Save and reopen</h2>
        <p>
          Pattern definitions are stored in the document model. Raster imports reference embedded
          document assets; vector definitions retain copied scene data; procedural definitions keep
          their recipe. The document codec includes the required pattern dependencies when encoding
          and decoding a document. Do not remove referenced embedded assets manually from a file.
        </p>
        <p>
          The current authoring UI supports renaming definitions and replacing a definition source
          with an imported raster tile. Replacement affects every linked use. Raster seam inspection
          can cyclically offset existing pixels and apply the result as a new embedded PNG; it does
          not synthesize seamless edges or provide wraparound painting. Finite expansion is not
          available. Those operations are not implied by the repeat preview.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Export status</h2>
        <p>
          Export Tile downloads the source cell as an SVG wrapper with its tile image embedded. The
          copied vector preview stays an SVG image, but its motif objects are not independently
          editable in the exported file. Export Supertile includes the full rectangular period for
          Grid and conventional Half-drop or Brick settings, with a clip at the supertile boundary;
          unsupported custom stagger fractions are rejected. Applied-fill SVG emits reusable
          vector pattern definitions for linked grid patterns on supported closed shapes. Unsupported
          arrangements, sources, stacks, blends, and alignment use an export-resolution raster
          fallback with a warning. PDF supports an axis-aligned grid with positive gaps; PDF/X-1a omits transparent tiles or non-opaque fills with a warning.
          Shifted arrangements, mirror, phase, and rotation are omitted from PDF with a warning.
          If a visible pattern has no resolved tile source, PDF and PDF/X export stops before
          writing an incomplete file; replace or reimport that tile first. Check the output limits
          in the <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/export')}>export guide</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          See the <a href={sitePath('/features/patterns')}>pattern feature status</a> for the current
          capability summary and limitations. General file output is covered in{' '}
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/export')}>Export &amp; Code Generation</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>
